at most free apps indeed don't make much money, and that's normal. We can also mention that the list of strategies is what successful ones use, but the reality is many don't succeed. Make it conversational and empathetic.
15:16:21 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re absolutely right that the reality doesn’t always match the “strategies” list. I think your observation actually points to a deeper truth: **most free apps don’t make any meaningful money at all**. What you’re seeing isn’t a failure of the business models—it’s the result of a few underappreciated facts: ### 1. The vast majority of free apps are basically hobbies or portfolio pieces A huge number of apps on the store are built by solo devs or tiny teams for fun, learning, or as a résumé project. They might spend a few weekends coding, throw on one ad banner that barely gets shown, and call it a day. They never expected a full income—maybe a tiny trickle of ad revenue or a donation, but nothing life-changing. For them, “monetization” is an afterthought, and the user sees exactly what you described: a clean app with almost no ads. ### 2. Many apps are funded by something else, not the app itself - **Big tech loss leaders**: Gmail, Google Maps, YouTube (free tier), Instagram, etc. don’t need in-app ads because they’re monetized indirectly (your data fuels their ad ecosystem, or they’re part of a larger platform strategy). The app is a vehicle for engagement, not a direct profit center. - **Venture-backed startups**: Those shiny free apps with no ads? Often burning investor cash to gain users. The goal is growth → acquisition. They deliberately avoid aggressive monetization to keep you happy and grow the user base. The real “profit” happens later when they’re bought out or when they finally turn on the monetization taps at scale. - **Cross-subsidization**: A company might offer a free app as a companion to a paid product (e.g., a free banking app that exists only because you have an account with them). ### 3. Monetization can be completely invisible to you You may see “barely any ads” because the app is: - **Silently collecting and selling your data** (with consent buried in a privacy policy). That’s very profitable and ad-free for you. - **Using affiliate links** in content you wouldn’t notice (e.g., a recipe app linking to grocery delivery for every ingredient). - **A freemium funnel** where only 1–3% of users ever pay, but they pay a lot. You might be one of the 97% who never sees the upsell because it’s a subtle “Pro” button in settings that you ignore. The developer still makes decent money off the few who convert. - **A white-label or open-source project** that the creator sells customized enterprise versions of, while the free version acts as marketing. ### 4. The “graveyard” effect You’re probably noticing the survivors. For every free app you’ve tried, there are thousands more that were abandoned with zero revenue after a year. Developers often stop updating because they can’t even cover the $99/year Apple Developer fee. Those apps do, indeed, sit there making nothing, and eventually they get delisted. ### 5. Ads aren’t always as obvious as billboards Some ad formats are so subtle you don’t register them as ads: - **Native content ads** (a “recommended” article that’s actually promoted) - **Sponsored search results** - **Partnership placements**, like a meditation app featuring a specific brand of tea in the background. Even when you do see a banner, the revenue per user is so tiny (often < $0.01 per day) that the app can afford to show it sparingly and still make pocket change if the user base is large. So, your gut feeling is largely correct: most free apps aren’t making “real money” for their creators. The strategies I listed earlier are what works *when* monetization is the actual goal and is executed professionally. But a majority of free apps you encounter are either hobby projects, thinly veiled data grabs, or loss leaders, which is why they appear to be sitting there with minimal visible advertising. It’s not that the creators are bad at the “fancy strategies”—it’s that they’re playing a different game entirely (or none at all).
